hey it's Michelle from love muffin  beauty science the nerdiest beauty nerd  on your screen right now today I'm going  to be discussing how to get vitamin D  without giving yourself too much skin  damage vitamin D is an essential  nutrient for your body and one of the  main sources of vitamin D for humans  it's exposing our skin to UVB radiation  from the Sun but of course UV and sun  exposure also causes skin cancer and  skin aging so we need to be careful  about sun protection we need sunscreen  hats protective clothing and we need to  remember to stay in the shade so what's  the right balance between getting enough  Sun for vitamin D and not having skin  cancer this topic is quite controversial  and there's tons and tons of opinions on  the internet  they aren't many clear guidelines from  medical authorities either so here's my  best attempt at untangling the  information that we have  [Music]  vitamin D is an important vitamin that  has lots of roles in your body it's  famous for maintaining calcium so your  bones don't get soft and you avoid  osteoporosis but it's involved in lots  of other things as well like heart  disease metabolism and preventing cancer  your skin produces vitamin D when it's  exposed to short wavelengths of UV  called UVB it happens with wavelengths  around 270 to 300 nanometers and it  happens the most efficiently at about  295 nanometers the amount of vitamin D  that your body produces when you go out  into the Sun depends on lots of things  like where you live the time of year the  time of day and the color of your skin  as well as making vitamin D in your skin  you can also get vitamin D from your  diet things like fatty fish liver cheese  mushrooms and foods that are specially  modified so that there's vitamin D added  to it  all of these can increase your body's  vitamin D but in general food only  contributes about 10 percent of your  total need of vitamin D you can also  take vitamin D supplements a lot of  people are vitamin D deficient so they  aren't getting enough through the Sun  and food it's something like a quarter  of Australians and 1/3 of people in the  u.s. who are all vitamin D deficient  [Music]  sunscreen blocks a lot of UVB from  entering your skin so it seems logical  to think that wearing sunscreens is  going to lower your vitamin D but a 2009  study found that wearing sunscreen  doesn't actually have too much of an  effect on your vitamin D levels that's  because most people don't apply enough  sunscreen and even if you try to apply  the right amount there are lots of spots  that you miss like your scalp between  your fingers around your eyes and these  spots get enough UV to make vitamin D  there's an interesting idea that you can  make a sunscreen that blocks most UV but  selectively lets in wavelengths of UV  that your skin uses to make vitamin D  the early sunscreen that I know off the  top of my head that does this is called  solar D and it lets in some rays that  are between 290 and 310 nanometers  I personally think this sort of  sunscreen is really going to take off in  the near future  [Music]  it's hard to give an exact figure in  terms of how many minutes of Sun  exposures you should get because of all  those things that I mentioned before  that will affect your vitamin D  production so things like where you live  the time of year the time of day and  what color your skin is  but there are some calculations that  have been done on the minimal  aerothermal dose or meed which is the  minimum amount of UV that makes your  skin go pink there's research that found  that exposing about a quarter of your  body's skin surface to half of the meed  two or three times a week is enough to  get adequate vitamin D synthesis you  also don't need to seem to need Sun all  that regularly for vitamin D your body  can rely on its storages of vitamin D  for a couple of months so you might be  able to get through some of winter  without sunshine different countries  have put out different guidelines for  sun exposure and vitamin D  supplementation so that you can get  enough vitamin D without risking too  much skin damage in the u.s. there  aren't any broad guidelines yet some  scientist suggests that around winter  you should try to get about 15 minutes  of Sun two or three times a week with  your face arms and legs exposed they  recommend that you do this in the middle  of the day when the UVB is highest so  around 11 a.m. till 3 p.m. this  guideline is for people with light  fitzpatrick true skin so if your skin  turns lighter then you need less sun and  if your skin tones darker you're going  to need more I really like the  guidelines given in Australia because  they're really clear and they were put  together in collaboration between people  who think that the Sun is bad  so the Australasian College of  dermatologists and the Cancer Council as  well as the people who think that  vitamin D deficiency is really bad so  the Australian and New Zealand bone a  mineral society The Endocrine Society of  Australia and osteoporosis Australia  these guidelines are also really recent  they were put together in January 2016  so they take into account newer research  on vitamin DS  importance these guidelines say when the  UV index is three or both in the middle  of the day which is most of the year in  Australia Sun Protection is recommended  when you're outdoors for more than a few  minutes so during this time most  Australians will still get enough  vitamin D from incidental exposure even  though they're taking sun protection  measures so even though they're wearing  sunscreen wearing sunglasses wearing  protective clothing wearing hats walking  between the car in the shops going  outside to buy a lunch that sort of  thing will give you enough sunshine to  get vitamin D then in late autumn and  winter when the UV index is below three  sun protection is not recommended and  they say that people should go outdoors  in the middle of the day with some skin  uncovered on most days of the week so  that's really nice and clear and because  it's based on the UV index you can  probably apply that in other places in  the world  just check the UV index for your  location and you can work out what to do  there are some apps that you can use to  do this so for example in Australia  there's the Sun smart app from the  Cancer Council in the UK it's often  cloudy and there's low UVB so in winter  the NHS recommends that almost everyone  should consider taking a vitamin D  supplement I've put links to all this  advice in the caption if you want to  read them in full  [Music]  so in addition to this advice there's a  few things that I want to add that you  should keep in mind sun exposure isn't  just UVB exposure  there's also longer wavelength UVA which  doesn't have any health benefits and  there's also infrared and visible light  that can also cause skin damage UVB is  highest in the middle of the day and  changes quite a lot during the day so  it's quite low in the morning and in the  evening but UVA levels are pretty  constant all throughout the day  sometimes of cost and benefit the middle  of the day seems to be the best time to  get the maximum vitamin D synthesis in  your skin while minimizing skin damage  from other sources  you only really need short exposures to  sunshine to make vitamin D it doesn't  mean that you have to bake in the Sun  for hours your skin slows down how much  vitamin D it makes after a while the  skin damage doesn't really slow down so  you're risking melanoma and other skin  cancers especially if you get sunburned  while you're trying to get vitamin D UVB  doesn't pass through glass so if you're  sitting in a patch of sunshine that's  coming in through a window you're not  going to make much vitamin D however UVA  those longer wavelengths they do pass  through glass which means you'll still  get skin damage tanning beds also  produce way more UVA than UVB so they're  worse than regular sunlight and they've  been definitely linked to skin cancer  if you're using anti-aging ingredients  in your skincare like alpha hydroxy  acids and vitamin A you should still  wear sunscreen since a lot of these can  increase your risk of sun damage if you  can't follow these guidelines or you're  particularly at risk of vitamin D  deficiency like of you or your family  members have had skin cancer if your  skin is really dark or if you're house  bound or if you're just concerned about  your vitamin D levels in general then  you should talk to your doctor about  checking your vitamin D levels and talk  about other options for increasing your  vitamin D so for example perhaps you can  take a supplement in summary small  periods of sun exposure in the middle of  the day will give you vitamin D while  minimizing your chances of skin cancer  and aging sunburn and UVA are still both  really bad and sunscreen is really  unlikely to make you more vitamin D  deficient than you already are  thanks for watching this video if you  liked my video I'd love it if you could  subscribe to my channel you can also  follow me on Facebook and Instagram for  lots and lots of science-based beauty  tips and you can also check out my blog  if you want to read more about beauty  science see you next time for more  nerdery  [Music]
 

